<title>Format notes with Markdown</title>

 <p>The text of an annotation can use Markdown. Headings, <em>emphasis</em>,
 <em>strong</em> text, <code>inline code</code>, fenced code blocks,
 strikethrough, bullet and numbered lists, task lists, block quotes, tables and
 horizontal rules are recognised. Links are shown underlined but cannot be
 clicked.</p>

 <p>The note itself is always edited as plain text: double-click a highlight
 to open its note and type the Markdown directly. The formatting is applied
 wherever the note is only displayed, that is in the tooltip that appears when
 you hover over the highlight and in the <gui>Annotations</gui> side pane.</p>

 <p>To see the notes exactly as written instead, press <key>H</key> while the
 document has the keyboard focus, or press the menu button in the top-right
 corner of the window and switch off <gui style="menuitem">Markdown
 Notes</gui>. A tooltip that is showing changes over immediately. Press
 <key>H</key> again to go back to the formatted view. The setting lasts until
 you close the window; every window starts with Markdown switched on.</p>

<note style="tip">
 <p>Plain notes look as they always did. Each line break is kept, four spaces
 at the start of a line do not turn it into a code block, and <code>&lt;</code>
 or <code>&amp;</code> are shown literally. Other PDF viewers show the note as
 the text you typed, Markdown marks included.</p>
</note>
